{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Interesting: Makefile works (cwd is the kernel tree root, VERSION = 7? That's strange—VERSION = 7 means kernel 7.x? Hmm, \"VERSION = 7\"—could this be a post-6.x mainline? Actually, did mainline become 6.17 → 7.0 in 2025? No wait, that's wrong… the kernel version for 2025–2026: 6.16 (Aug 2025), 6.17 (Nov 2025), 7.0 in early 2026? Plausible, given that the current date is 2026-07-24. Right, mainline was renamed to 7.0 in early 2026 (after 6.17 came 7.x? Actually Linus announced the 7.0 release). Okay.)\n\nBut README.md doesn't exist? The kernel tree has no README.md at the root… right, the kernel tree has no README.md at the root (it's a \"README\" file). No problem.\n\nBut earlier it said drivers/usb/typec/ucsi.c doesn't exist?? Weird. Unless the path resolution uses a different base. The fact that Makefile was found at the cwd means cwd = kernel root. So /workspace/linux is the kernel root. Then why does drivers/usb/typec/ucsi.c return \"No such file\"? Maybe rtk's path resolution treats the path differently—it strips the leading component? \"/Makefile\" failed but \"Makefile\" succeeded => cwd-based. \"usr/bin/grep\" failed => there's no usr directory under cwd (reasonable). Then maybe drivers/ doesn't exist in the tree?? Or maybe in this kernel's version, the usb/typec files have moved? In kernel 7.x, drivers/usb/typec should still exist. Let me test \"drivers/Makefile\" and \"drivers/usb/typec/Kconfig\"."}}
