{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"There's no kernfs in the typec drivers of 7.x. That is, in this tree, the WARN path has been removed — probably this refactoring is the upstream fix itself. But wait — the user's 6.17 OEM kernel does hit the WARN. And the name being removed is 'typec'. In the 6.x kernel, who was in the typec path calling kernfs_remove_by_name_ns?\n\nLet me try to remember 6.17's drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c... I don't think there's any kernfs in 6.17 either. Hmm. Then where does \"remove 'typec'\" come from?\n\nLet me rethink: maybe the WARN is being triggered not from a direct call in typec, but from a sysfs cleanup via device_del: `device_add_groups`/`device_remove_groups` → sysfs_remove_group → sysfs_remove_file → kernfs_remove (not the by_name_ns with WARN). But does sysfs_remove_file_group use kernfs_remove_by_name_ns? Actually:\n\nfs/sysfs/group.c: sysfs_remove_group → for each attribute, sysfs_remove_file → kernfs_remove (by pointer, no WARN).\n\nfs/sysfs/dir.c? sysfs_rename_target... `sysfs_remove_file_ns`?\n\nHmm, but WARN at kernfs/dir.c:1706 in kernel 6.17. Let me try to remember the kernel 6.17 kernfs dir.c. In 6.17:\n\n```c\nstatic struct kernfs_node *__kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(struct kernfs_node *parent,\n\t\t\t\t\t\t      const char *name,\n\t\t\t\t\t\t      struct ns_wrapper ns)\n{\n\tstruct kernfs_node *dir, *found;\n\n\tlockdep_assert_held(&kernfs_idr_lock);\n\n\tdir = parent->sd? ...\n\tfound = kernfs_find_and_get_by_name(parent, name, ns);\n\tif (!found) {\n\t\tWARN(1, KERN_WARNING \"kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\\n\", name);\n\t\treturn ERR_PTR(-ENOENT);\n\t}\n\t...\n}\n\nstruct kernfs_node *\nkernfs_remove_by_name_ns(struct kernfs_node *parent,\n\t\t\t  const char *name, struct ns_wrapper ns)\n{\n\tstruct kernfs_node *kn;\n\n\tmutex_lock(&kernfs_idr_lock);\n\tkn = __kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(parent, name, ns);\n\tmutex_unlock(&kernfs_idr_lock);\n\n\treturn