on connector change (unplug): ucsi_handle_connector_change → if !typec_port_get... then ucsi_unregister_partner(con).\n\nSo where does the \"typec\" attribute come from? Is it in the typec port's class? Wait — a device attribute named exactly \"typec\"... let me recall drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c:\n\n```c\nstatic ssize_t typec_cc_show...\n```\n\nNo, that's not it.\n\nLet me think about Dell's specific bug. Dell Pro (Precision 5) 16 — OEM kernel 6.17.0-1011-oem (Canonical OEM kernel). Is this known LKML issue from 2025–2026? \"kernfs: can not remove 'typec', no directory\" — I feel like there was a 2025 patch by Heikki Krogerus: \"typec: ucsi: fix partner registration race\", or maybe UCSI's \"connector change\" handling registers the partner while the port doesn't exist yet, and then unregister tries to remove... but the name is 'typec'.\n\nHmm, maybe I should search upstream commits. But this environment has no network, only rtk. I'll have to rely on memory + what's in the tree.\n\nActually — wait. Maybe the \"typec\" attribute: the typec core (6.17) drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c:\n\n```c\nstatic struct device_type typec_partner_type? \n```\n\nHmm. Or: is it a typec \"port\" device that has a \"typec\" file under /sys/class/typec? No.\n\nDifferent thought: the attribute is \"typec\" on a ucsi connector's class device? UCSI creates... actually, ucsi.c has a debugfs, not sysfs.\n\nOK, another approach: maybe the partner's dev directory: dev_set_name(\"%s-partner-%d\") — the name would be \"typec-partner-0\", not \"typec\". The WARN prints the name passed to kernfs_remove_by_name_ns = attribute name = \"typec\". So somewhere device_remove_file(dev, attr with name \"typec\") exists, or kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(dev_kobj, \"typec\").\n\nWhat in a typec port device's sysfs directory is a file named \"typec\"? Hmm... /sys/class/typec/typec0 has \"typec\" → no.\n\nWait, what about UCSI: ucsi.c has this:\n\n```c\nstatic ssize_t ucsi_pdo_show? \n```\n\nNo.\n\nLet me try to remember actual 6.17 code of the typec core's partner API (drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c? no — was it split in 6.7? Is the partner code in drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c? Let me recall the actual 6.17 file listing: drivers/usb/typec/: anx7411.c, connector.c? (no, that's the old typec connector class)... 6.17: altmodes/, class? Hmm, I don't think class.c existed in 6.17. In the 6.x listing: drivers/usb/typec/{hd3ss3220.c, port.h, typec.h, tcpm/, ucsi/, tipd/, mux/, altmodes/, anx7411.c, rt1719.c, stusb160x.c, wusb3801.c, typec/...}. Actually, where are the implementations of typec_register_partner/unregister? In 6.x it was drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c — no wait, I think in 6.x it was at top level as \"drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c\" and in 6.15 it was moved to tcpm/tcpm.c.\n\nAnd in 7.x (this tree) the files have been further reorganized: class.c, bus.c, pd.c, mux.c, retimer.c, mode_selection.c, port-mapper.c.\n\nNow — the WARN in 6.17 OEM: the 6.17 OEM kernel is fairly close to the mainline 6.17 code. So I can reason about the 6.17 code.\n\nLet me recall 6.17 tcpm.c's partner functions:\n\n```c\nstruct typec_partner {\n\tstruct device dev;\n\tstruct list_head node;\n\tenum typec_pwr_role pr;\n\tenum typec_data_role dr;\n\tenum typec_cc_role cc;\n\tenum typec_polarity polarity;\n\tunsigned long capabilities;\n\tstruct typec_altmode *altmodes;\n\tint num_altmodes;\n\tbool is_rtd;\n};\n\nint typec_partner_register(struct typec_port *port, struct typec_partner_desc *desc)\n{\n\tstruct typec_partner *partner = NULL;\n\tconst struct typec_altmode *adev;\n\tint ret, i;\n\n\tlockdep_assert_held(&port->lock);\n\n\tpartner = kzalloc(sizeof(*partner), GFP_KERNEL);\n\tif (!partner)\n\t\treturn -ENODEV;\n\n\tpartner->cc = desc->cc;\n\tpartner->polarity = desc->polarity;\n\tpartner->is_rtd = desc->is_rtd;\n\tpartner->dev.bus = &typec_bus_type;\n\tpartner->dev.type = &typec_partner_type;\n\tpartner->dev.parent = port->dev.parent;\n\tpartner->cap.pr = TYPEC_NO_PR;\n\tpartner->cap.dr = TYPEC_NO_DR;\n\tpartner->cap.usb = 0;\n\n\tif (desc->pwr_role != TYPEC_NO_PR) ...\n\n\tpartner->altmodes = kzalloc(array_size(size_t, desc->num_altmodes), GFP_KERNEL);\n\tif (!partner->altmodes) {\n\t\tkfree(partner);\n\t\treturn -ENOMEM;\n\t}\n\n\tfor (i = 0; i < desc->num_altmodes; i++) {\n\t\tadev = &desc->altmodes[i];\n\t\tret = typec_partner_register_altmode(port, partner, adev, i);\n\t\t...\n\t}\n\n\tpartner->num_altmodes = i;\n\tlist_add_tail(&partner->node, &port->partners);\n\treturn 0;\n}\n```\n\nAnd typec_partner_plug:\n\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_plug(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\tpartner->dev.parent = partner->port->dev.parent?? \n\tret = dev_set_name(&partner->dev, \"%s-partner-%d\",\n\t\t\t   dev_name(partner->port->dev), partner->port->id++);\n\tif (!ret) {\n\t\tdev_set_name(...);\n\t\tret = device_register? \n```\n\nHmm, I recall that the partner's device_add happened inside typec_partner_plug, with a parent symlink to the port:\n\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_plug(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\tint ret;\n\n\tret = dev_set_name(&partner->dev, \"%s-partner-%d\",\n\t\t\t   dev_name(partner->port->dev), partner->port->id++);\n\tif (ret < 0)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\tpartner->dev.parent = partner->port->dev.parent;\n\tpartner->dev.bus = &typec_bus_type;\n\tpartner->dev.type = &typec_partner_type;\n\tret = device_add(&partner->dev);\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nThen what about \"typec_register_port\"? Port devices created via class_create(\"typec\")... /sys/class/typec/typec0. The class \"typec\" — and dev_kobj(&port->dev) is... hmm! `typec_register_partner` might have added a SYMLINK in the PORT's sysfs directory, named by the partner's dev name, under a \"partners\" directory? No.\n\nLet me focus on the attribute name \"typec\". In 6.17, who calls `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(x, \"typec\")`? device_remove_file(dev, &dev_attr_name=\"typec\"). What device has a device_attribute named \"typec\"?\n\nI got it: the TYPEC PORT itself! In 6.17's tcpm.c... no. Or the UCSI connector? ucs