{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Regex is working (class.c has two instances of sysfs_create_link, and in addition to the two remove_link instances, there are also the create instances for the reverse-direction link and cable). Next, identify the function names. In the 6.17 kernel, this code exists in drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c? Hmm wait, was the partner registration in 6.17 in tcpm.c? Let's try to recall 6.17 more concretely... Actually, I strongly recall that in 6.x it was in drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c:\n\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_plug(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\tstruct typec_port *port = partner->port;\n\tint ret;\n\n\tlockdep_assert_held(&port->lock);\n\t...\n\tpartner->dev.parent = port->dev.parent;\n\t...\n\tdev_set_name(&partner->dev, \"%s-partner-%d\", dev_name(&port->dev), port->next_port_id++);\n\t...\n\tret = device_add(&partner->dev);\n\tif (ret) {\n\t\tdev_warn(&partner->dev, \"partner device add failed: %d\\n\", ret);\n\t\ttypec_partner_register_fail...\n\t}\n\tlist_add_tail(&partner->node, &port->partners);\n\t...\n\tsysfs_notify? \n}\n\nstatic void typec_partner_unplug(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\t...\n\tdevice_unregister(&partner->dev);\n}\n```\n\nHmm — where exactly was the \"typec\" symlink created? Now I remember! The \"typec\" symlink was added in a 2024 patch: commit 0e4533c7b33e? \"typec: Create a 'typec' symlink in partner's device directory\"? There was a real 2024 upstream commit by Heikki Krogerus: \"typec: Add partner sysfs link to port\" — it creates a symlink in the partner's dev directory named \"typec\" pointing to the port, to make partner → port discoverable (since the parent is set to port->dev.parent, which could be a PCI device, etc.). So the partner's sysfs looks like: /sys/bus/typec/devices/typec0-partner-0/typec -> /sys/class/typec/typec0 (or /sys/devices/.../port). That's right! I remember now: commit 2b2f1eac0590? \"typec: tcpm: Add partner to port sysfs link\"? Hmm.\n\nActually, in 6.17 there's a symlink at /sys/class/typec/typec0-partner-*/typec? And the class \"typec\" is...\n\nNow, the WARN: on disconnect, ucsi_unregister_partner → typec_unregister_partner → typec_partner_unplug → device_unregister(&partner->dev). sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \"typec\") — where is it called in 6.17? I think in 6.17 that removal was inside typec_unregister_partner:\n\nLet's look at the actual 6.17 typec_unregister_partner in tcpm.c... hmm, but which file is it in 6.17? Was the partner API file in 6.17 drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c? No wait — actually I think the partner API lived in drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c (which also contains the TCPM driver functions), and 6.17 has that structure. Hmm, but then in 7.x it got split off into class.c.\n\nLet me reconstruct 6.17's typec_unregister_partner from memory (fairly confident):\n\n```c\nvoid typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\tstruct typec_port *port = partner->port;\n\n\tlockdep_assert_held(&port->lock);\n\tif (!partner)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\ttypec_altmode_unregister? (loop over altmodes)\n\t...\n\tif (sysfs_link?) ...\n\tlist_del(&partner->node);\n\tpartner->port = NULL;\n\ttypec_partner_unplug(partner);\n}\n```\n\nAnd typec_partner_unplug:\n\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_unplug(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\tdevice_unattach_ag? ...\n\tsysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, \"typec\");   /* << here */\n\tdevice_unregister(&partner->dev);\n}\n```\n\nRoot cause of ENOENT: typec_partner_plug does device_add, and on failure... actually the failure mode is: `typec_partner_plug` failed partway through (device_add failed → dev_kobj directory doesn't exist), but the \"typec\" symlink creation succeeded (sysfs_create_link before device_add, or independent of it)? In that case, on unplug, sysfs_remove_link fails with ENOENT → WARN via device_remove... no wait, sysfs_remove_link itself uses kernfs_find and returns -ENOENT without WARN. A WARN means the cal