lying on memory. The 6.17 code probably contains:\n- drivers/usb/typec/ucsi.c? Or ucsi/ucsi.c? The user's stack has ucsi_unregister_partner -> ucsi_handle_connector_change. In 6.17: drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c? I'm fairly confident the ucsi/ subdirectory was introduced in 6.14 (commit \"typec: move ucsi driver to own directory\"). And the partner code in 6.17: \"class.c\"? Hmm, if the reorganization landed in 6.15/6.16, then 6.17 also has class.c. The user is saying \"identify the exact problematic function in drivers/usb/typec (and drivers/usb/typec/ucsi)\" with file:line within \"this tree\" — so the tree IS the reference. Alright — I'll describe it all in this tree's layout and note the correspondence with 6.17 (ucsi_unregister_partner in ucsi/ucsi.c, typec_unregister_partner in class.c).\n\nSo let me extract the body of the relevant function in this tree:\n\n1. ucsi_unregister_partner + ucsi_handle_connector_change (ucsi/ucsi.c) — I need the line numbers: I'll use the technique of making a grep that has a \"see remaining\" (>25) with a pattern covering those regions. Or: grep ucsi.c with a pattern that only matches in that region and yields 26+ matches? Not easy.\n\nAlternative to get line numbers without >25: run a grep, then search the TEE directory with a guess? No.\n\nHmm wait — how about using `grep -n`? The rtk tool wraps /usr/bin/grep. The \"path\" is the file. What if the PATTERN contained something that makes grep show more? No.\n\nHow does rtk decide the display? Maybe the display includes line numbers when matches are 25 or fewer? Looking at earlier 25-or-fewer displays: \"Makefile\" (30 matches — no numbers), \"partner in pd.c\" (2 matches, no numbers), \"typec_unregister_partner in ucsi.c\" (1 match, no numbers). No numbers anywhere in the display.\n\nNew idea: pattern-matching a unique line to get the line number by comparing multiple small greps — overkill.\n\nLet me use the >25 technique cleverly. To identify the body of typec_unregister_partner in class.c: construct a regex matching lines between \"typec_unregister_partner\" and \"EXPORT..(typec_unregister_partner)\". E.g., pattern \"partner\" matches 250, with first 25 displayed; the numbered log contains all 250 — I can only read the first 25 of the log... The log file contains all 250 lines with numbers, but rtk's display of that log is truncated to 25. However, grepping that log with a more specific pattern returns a new numbered set! E.g., grep the 250-line log with pattern \"typec_unregister_partner\" → numbered lines (log line → class.c line). And grep that log with \"sysfs_remove\" etc. So: use a wide pattern (250 matches), then query the log's contents with specific patterns to get the numbered lines of interest!\n\nSo: grep class.c for \"partner\" → log L (250 matches, numbered but only displayable as first 25). Then grep L for \"typec_unregister_partner\" (matches: comment, function definition, EXPORT) → numbered lines in class.c! Then grep L for \"sysfs_remove_link\" etc.\n\nBut I need the log L's name. The name is shown by \"see remaining\" in the display of the first grep (250 > 25).\n\nEarlier: class.c \"partner\" grep's display had \"see remaining: 1787788690_grep_0_drivers_usb_typec_class_c.log\". That's my log L! (250 matches). Read via pattern... Let me grep that log for \"unregister_partner\", \"sysfs_\", \"link_device\"."}}
