{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Very interesting! In this tree:\n- typec_partner_link_device(partner, dev): around ~888-904: creates a \"typec\" link inside dev (usb2/usb3 device or the attached device's kobj) pointing to partner, and additionally creates a reverse-direction link. Line 892: sysfs_create_link(&dev->kobj, &partner->dev.kobj, \"typec\") — the link is created on USB (usb2/usb3) device's kobj and named \"typec\"!\n- typec_unregister_partner (line 1174-1196): around ~1185/1189 it calls typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb2_dev/usb3_dev), then device_unregister(&partner->dev).\n\nSo the name of the 'typec' symlink is in the sysfs directory of the USB2/USB3 device! That's it — it's a known feature: /sys/class/usb*/... \"typec\" symlink? Actually, in the real mainline 6.17, drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c or class.c had something like:\n\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_link_device(struct typec_partner *partner, struct device *dev)\n{\n\tif (device_is_registered(dev)) ...\n\tret = sysfs_create_link(&dev->kobj, &partner->dev.kobj, \"typec\");\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nHmm wait — actually I'm now remembering a 2025 commit by Heikki: \"typec: Add 'typec' symlink to USB device\" — adding a \"typec\" symlink to the USB device (usb2/usb3) so you can find the typec partner from the USB device, and further ucsi uses this... And the known bug: at connector-change time, typec_unregister_partner runs, and while the USB device's kobj has not (yet) been created / has already been removed (because the USB device gets disconnected at the same moment), it calls sysfs_remove_link — the usb2_dev's kobj directory no longer exists → kernfs ENOENT → WARN.\n\nActually — the root cause is precisely this: the partner gets unregistered while the USB device is being removed in parallel, or a port's usb2_dev is NULL... Let's look at the body of typec_unregister_partner (1174-1196). From the log:\n- 1159: typec_partner_link_device(partner, port->usb2_dev);\n- 1161: typec_partner_link_device(partner, port->usb3_dev);\n(these are inside typec_register_partner)\n- 1185: typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb2_dev);\n- 1189: typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb3_dev);\n(inside unregister)\n\nLet's grab the body of typec_partner_unlink_device (906-…). From the sysfs log: line 908 sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, dev_name(dev)) — this is probably the error path of link_device (removes the partner-side link on failure), or maybe unlink? The function definitions are: 888 link_device, 906 unlink_device. Wait, only 17 lines between the start of the 2 functions? 888-904 = link, 906-... = unlink. So:\n\n888: static void typec_partner_link_device(struct typec_partner *partner, struct device *dev)\n889: {\n890: int ret;\n891:\n892: ret = sysfs_create_link(&dev->kobj, &partner->dev.kobj, \"typec\");\n893: if (!ret) { ... }  hmm\n894:\n895: ...\n896: ret = sysfs_create_link(&partner->dev.kobj, &dev->kobj, dev_name(dev));\n897: if (ret) {\n898:     sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \"typec\");\n899:     return;\n900: }\n901: ...\n904: }\n905:\n906: static void typec_partner_unlink_device(struct typec_partner *partner, struct device *dev)\n907: {\n908:     sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, dev_name(dev));\n909:     sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \"typec\");\n910: }\n\nYes! Line 909 must be sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \"typec\") — in the partner grep log it wasn't listed under \"sysfs_\"... wait, the sysfs_ query returned lines including 908 but did not list 909 → but the log from 898 has 2 \"sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj...\" — one at 898, and the other is... in the first sysfs grep, after \"sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, dev_name(dev));\" the display continued with \"+26 more\" — and in the query, in addition to 908, was \"class.c:898:\" shown? Actually the sysfs_ query output only listed 908 for remove_link and did not list 898! Because the sysfs_ grep's log (51 matches) — query results: 735, 892, 896, 908, 969, 972, 1033, 1037, 2297 — 898 wasn't there?? But the previous sysfs_ grep display had 2 hits for \"sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \\\"typec\\\");\" (898 and 909?). The query to 1787788690 (the PARTNER log) returned what was included in the \"sysfs_\" lines of the \"partner\" log? No wait — 1787788690 is the log of the \"partner\" grep (250 matches). So the sysfs_ lines listed there = lines containing \"partner\" and \"sysfs_\": 735, 892, 896, 908, 969… 898 (`sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \"typec\");`) — does this line contain \"partner\"? No — only dev->kobj. 909 also has no \"partner\". OK, consistent.\n\nSo in typec_partner_unlink_device (this tree, 906-910):\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_unlink_device(struct typec_partner *partner, struct device *dev)\n{\n\tsysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, dev_name(dev));\n\tsysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \"typec\");\n}\n```\n\nNow: does it guard for dev == NULL or !device_is_registered? The 250-line log of partner... Query the log lines 906-910 with the \"partner\" pattern? Those don't have it in common.\n\nSo let's query the PARTNER log for the body of typec_unregister_partner: grep the log with \"port->usb\" and \"device_is_registered\" and \"if (\":"}}
