{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Interesting! There's `mutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock)` — a dedicated lock for the link operation! In 6.17 (mainline before the fix), this lock probably didn't exist, and the sysfs link creation/deletion raced with the USB device removal. Hmm, wait — but actually, let me reconsider: was the \"typec\" symlink feature, and the partner_link_lock, added as a FIX by an upstream commit? Let me try to remember:\n\nThe 2025 upstream commit I now clearly recall:\n- \"typec: Add a 'typec' entry to USB devices\" (adding a \"typec\" symlink to /sys/class/usb*/…) — was that commit 83cbb0a1c74b? By Heikki Krogerus, February 2025?\n- \"typec: ucsi: Fix ...\"\n\nHmm. But is the WARN in the user's 6.17 OEM kernel — that kernel has those \"typec\" symlinks (otherwise you can't get this WARN) — so the feature existed in 6.17 mainline. In 6.17, was there a partner_link_lock, or a guard in unlink?\n\nLet me look at typec_unregister_partner in mainline 6.17... the 6.17 file is: drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c? Or class.c? This 7.x tree has class.c, and the user's OEM build 6.17.0-1011-oem — OEM kernels track mainline + Ubuntu patches, and if class.c exists in mainline 6.17, then the 6.17 OEM has it too. When did the typec split into class.c happen? I think there was a series in 2024 like \"typec: Move ... to separate file\" (Heikki). Honestly I don't remember, but the 6.17 OEM might be quite different.\n\nThat said, what the user asked is: root cause + whether the fix is in \"this tree\" or upstream. So I should:\n1. Show the buggy chain in the 6.17 OEM context (equivalent to this tree, or an ancestor of it).\n2. Check whether this tree contains guards: it does contain partner_link_lock — need to check whether unlink has a `device_is_registered()`/NULL check, and how the creation path avoids creating a link to an unregistered dev.\n\nGet the body of class.c lines 880–912 (link/unlink) and 1140–1196 (register/unregister). Query the class.c oracle with a pattern that matches those lines — but the oracle only has lines containing \"partner\". Lines 888–910 and 1140–1196: most contain \"partner\" (the function parameters) ✓, but e.g. line 909 `sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \"typec\");` doesn't → unknown, but the pattern can be inferred.\n\nLet me query the oracle log with a \"9[0-9][0-9]:\" pattern? Line numbers: the pattern \"89:\" won't work due to the 4-digit issue... line 892: \"c:892:\" contains \"89\" + \"2:\" — the pattern \"c:89\" matches 890–899! The pattern \"c:89\" also matches \"1892:\" — \"c:1892\" contains \"1892\" — does \"c:89\" appear? \"c:1892\" — substring \"c:1\" nope; after \"c:\" comes \"1\". OK, safe for line 89x: \"c:89\" matches only 890–899 (and 8900+ but out of range). Similarly \"c:90\" → 900–909, \"c:11[0-9]\"? The pattern \"c:11\" matches 1100–1199 (but only lines within the oracle)."}}
