{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"I've got a handle on the structure. The \"typec\" symlink in class.c:892/896/908 is on usb2/usb3's `dev` (the generic dev). This is different from 6.17 (mainline) — actually mainline 6.17 has the same code in class.c: typec_partner_link_device was added in 2024 (commit 6e8eaa720c18? \"typec: Link partner to USB device\"?) — I think in 6.17 it existed in drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c? No wait: in 6.17, where was typec_register_partner? The user's stack has typec_unregister_partner (name unchanged).\n\nThe key question here is: when does ENOENT happen? typec_partner_unlink_device does the following:\n- 908: sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, dev_name(dev))\n- 909?: sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \"typec\")  ← 'typec' WARN\n\nThe WARN says 'typec' → the failing call = line 909 (2nd removal), with parent = &dev->kobj → dev = port->usb2_dev or usb3_dev. For the parent dentry not to be a directory / not to have the child, dev's kobj directory must have already disappeared or never existed → the USB device was removed (device_del) before the partner's unregister, or at the same time... but wait, line 1185 of typec_unregister_partner: guarded by `if (port->usb2_dev)`. If the USB device had been properly cleared (port->usb2_dev=NULL at disconnect), it would be skipped. The WARN indicates the pointer was still non-NULL and its kobj was gone → the clearing of port->usb2_dev happens elsewhere (typec_usb2_remove? or via dev removal), but the usb device removal path raced with the partner unregistration (ucsi workqueue).\n\nWhere are the setters? In the tree grep: `port->usb2_dev = dev;` and `port->usb2_dev = NULL;` (\"if (port->usb2_dev == dev) port->usb2_dev = NULL;\") — inside functions like typec_register_usb2_port? Grep for \"typec_set_usb\" or the function names:"}}
