{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"So, here's how it works in 7.x:\n\n**USB port object** (drivers/usb/core/port.c): each USB port has its own device (port_dev), and when a Type-C connector device is bound (component driver framework: component_add → bind), it does the following:\n- Creates \"connector\" symlink (bidirectional) between the USB port device and the typec connector (typec) device\n- Sets port_dev->connector\n- If child (USB device) is already present: typec_attach(connector, &child->dev)\n- connector_unbind removes the links, and probably calls typec_deattach? — grepping \"typec_deattach\" in port.c: no match! Hmm — so unbind doesn't deattach?? Wait, maybe deattach happens in usb core's disconnect path via a different route…… or there's a bug in that part. Whatever.\n\nSo in 7.x the typec_connector device — a DEVICE (with kobj)! And typec_partner_attach(struct typec_connector *con, struct device *dev) uses container_of(con, ...) — in this tree, `struct typec_connector` is embedded: \"port->con\" — is typec_port->con embedded in the typec PORT struct? And it also has its own kobject? \"connector_ops\" binds the USB port with the typec connector device; port->con must have dev/kobj. Hmm, \"struct typec_port *port = container_of(con, struct typec_...)\" — 2146: \"struct typec_port *port = container_of(con, struct typec_port, con);\" — so typec_port contains `struct typec_connector con;`, and the CONNECTOR's device is probably `&port->dev` itself?? Then the \"typec connector\" device in sysfs is the PORT's device itself (e.g., /sys/class/typec/typec0), and the USB port creates a \"connector\" symlink pointing to it.\n\nOK. That's the design in the 7.x tree. Next: the question is whether the WARN from 6.17 OEM is present or fixed in this tree.\n\nKey comparison: what did 6.17 have? The user's kernel 6.17.0-1011-oem: OEM builds are from mainline 6.17.x. So in mainline 6.17:\n- drivers/usb/typec/class.c? — probably exists (class.c containing typec_register_partner, \"typec\" symlinks, usb2_dev/usb3_dev in port), and the USB core is the older one (devices.c/port.c don't exist in 6.17 — those are new in 7.x; in 6.17, who set usb2/usb3_dev?).\n\nHmm wait — the \"typec\" symlinks on usb devices + \"connector\" symlinks + companion framework — I now strongly recall the actual upstream series! 2025-11 (6.18/6.19 or 7.x): USB core \"usb: core: Add USB port object\"? Hmm. And the typec side: \"typec: Add typec_connector\"? …\n\nHonestly, what I now strongly suspect is: this tree's layout itself IS the upstream fix series for exactly this Dell class of bug — the partner/usb-link mechanism was changed. Because in 6.17 (user's kernel), the mechanism was the older one: in 6.17's drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c (or class.c? whichever), typec_partner_plug/unplug:\n\nActually, let me try to recall the 6.17 partner code — I have a decent memory of 6.17's drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c. In 6.17:\n\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_plug(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\tstruct typec_port *port = partner->port;\n\tint ret;\n\n\tlockdep_assert_held(&port->lock);\n\n\tret = dev_set_name(&partner->dev, \"%s-partner-%d\",\n\t\t\t   dev_name(&port->dev), port->id++);\n\tif (ret < 0)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\tpartner->dev.parent = port->dev.parent?? \n\tpartner->dev.bus = &typec_bus_type;\n\tpartner->dev.type = &typec_partner_type;\n\tpartner->dev.groups = NULL; (usb_pd_id_groups?)\n\tret = device_add(&partner->dev);\n\tif (ret) { partner->dev.bus = NULL; dev_warn(...); }\n\tlist_add_tail(&partner->node, &port->partners);\n\tsysfs_notify? \n}\n```\n\nAnd 6.17's typec_unregister_partner (tcpm.c):\n\n```c\nvoid typec_unregister_partner(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\tu64 modes;\n\n\tlockdep_assert_held(&partner->port->lock);\n\tif (!partner)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\t/*\n\t * If there's a USB connection to the partner, then disconnect it.\n\t */\n\t...\n\ttypec_partner_unplug(partner);\n}\n```\n\nUgh. I really can't remember whether 6.17 had usb2_dev. The user's WARN proves 6.17 had something named \"typec\" whose removal could fail: either (a) the typec partner device on usb device sysfs (usb2/usb3 link), or (b) a sysfs file named \"typec\" on a partner or port device.\n\nHmm — actually, wait. What about a simpler possibility: in 6.17 the partner's sysfs tree contains a file…… no.\n\nLet me think about \"kernfs: can not remove 'typec'\". If the code is 6.17 and typec_unregister_partner did \"sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, \"typec\")\"? — i.e., a \"typec\" symlink in the PARTNER directory pointing to the port, created in typec_partner_plug at device_add time…… then on unplug, if the partner's dentry is already gone, ENOENT — when does that happen? If two concurrent calls to typec_unregister_partner? No, the second would free……\n\nHmm, or: the \"typec\" symlink was created in the PARTNER's directory during register, and on the first unplug attempt, device_del failed halfway leaving the kobj in a weird state, and……\n\nCan't nail down 6.17 exactly without the source. But — I can nail down the mechanism from the 7.x tree (the fix tree): the \"typec\" link is on the USB port's child device's kobj (dev->kobj with dev = USB device), and removal is done in typec_partner_unlink_device (906-913).\n\nRace in 7.x (still present? or fixed?):\n- USB port.c's connector_bind: typec_attach(...) → typec_partner_attach: under partner_link_lock, usbX_dev = dev, link.\n- typec_unregister_partner: under partner_link_lock: if usbX_dev { unlink; NULL }.\n- Device removal path: usb core disconnects child → who calls deattach? I didn't see a typec_deattach call in port.c — hmm, but \"typec_deattach\" grep across the whole tree: who calls it? Let me grep usb.c/hub.c/devices.c. If nobody calls deattach before usb child device removal, then port->usb2_dev can dangle after USB device removal…… but then typec_unregister_partner would unlink from the dead device → exactly the WARN!! But wait — in 7.x, partner_link_lock protects against USB-side attach/detach, not against the usb device removal itself. However, if deattach is called by hub.c at disconnect (before device del), then pointer gets cleared and unlink happens while kobj is alive → no WARN.\n\nLet me grep hub.c/devices.c/usb.c for typec_deattach:"}}
