 can't remember whether 6.17 had usb2_dev. The user's WARN proves 6.17 had something named \"typec\" whose removal could fail: either (a) the typec partner device on usb device sysfs (usb2/usb3 link), or (b) a sysfs file named \"typec\" on a partner or port device.\n\nHmm — actually, wait. What about a simpler possibility: in 6.17 the partner's sysfs tree contains a file…… no.\n\nLet me think about \"kernfs: can not remove 'typec'\". If the code is 6.17 and typec_unregister_partner did \"sysfs_remove_link(&partner->dev.kobj, \"typec\")\"? — i.e., a \"typec\" symlink in the PARTNER directory pointing to the port, created in typec_partner_plug at device_add time…… then on unplug, if the partner's dentry is already gone, ENOENT — when does that happen? If two concurrent calls to typec_unregister_partner? No, the second would free……\n\nHmm, or: the \"typec\" symlink was created in the PARTNER's directory during register, and on the first unplug attempt, device_del failed halfway leaving the kobj in a weird state, and……\n\nCan't nail down 6.17 exactly without the source. But — I can nail down the mechanism from the 7.x tree (the fix tree): the \"typec\" link is on the USB port's child device's kobj (dev->kobj with dev = USB device), and removal is done in typec_partner_unlink_device (906-913).\n\nRace in 7.x (still present? or fixed?):\n- USB port.c's connector_bind: typec_attach(...) → typec_partner_attach: under partner_link_lock, usbX_dev = dev, link.\n- typec_unregister_partner: under partner_link_lock: if usbX_dev { unlink; NULL }.\n- Device removal path: usb core disconnects child → who calls deattach? I didn't see a typec_deattach call in port.c — hmm, but \"typec_deattach\" grep across the whole tree: who calls it? Let me grep usb.c/hub.c/devices.c. If nobody calls deattach before usb child device removal, then port->usb2_dev can dangle after USB device removal…… but then typec_unregister_partner would unlink from the dead device → exactly the WARN!! But wait — i