{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The body of deattach (2168~2187, lines containing \"port\" have been confirmed; line 2177's unlink contains partner but not \"port\" — from the earlier \"link\" oracle: line 2177 has typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, dev)); probably:\n\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_deattach(struct typec_connector *con, struct device *dev)\n{\n\tstruct typec_port *port = container_of(con, struct typec_port, con);\n\tstruct typec_partner *partner;\n\n\tmutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);\n\tpartner = typec_get_partner(port);\n\tif (partner)\n\t\ttypec_partner_unlink_device(partner, dev);   // 2176-2177\n\tif (port->usb2_dev == dev)\n\t\tport->usb2_dev = NULL;                       // 2181-2182\n\telse if (port->usb3_dev == dev)\n\t\tport->usb3_dev = NULL;                       // 2183-2184\n\tmutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);\n}\n```\n\nAnd attach (2143~2167):\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_attach(struct typec_connector *con, struct device *dev)\n{\n\tstruct typec_port *port = container_of(con, struct typec_port, con);\n\tstruct typec_partner *partner;\n\tbool is_usb3 = is_usb3? (dev...);\n\n\tif (is_usb3?) {\n\t\t...\n\t}\n\tmutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);\n\tport->usb2_dev = dev;     // 2154\n\t... (2155-2156)\n\tport->usb3_dev = dev;     // 2157\n\tpartner = typec_get_partner(port);\n\tif (partner)                          // 2161?\n\t\ttypec_partner_link_device(partner, dev);  // 2163\n\tmutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);  // 2166\n}\n```\n\nNow — the key point — is the fix for the user's 6.17 bug in this tree? Comparison with 6.17: in 6.17 (the user's kernel), the flow had no partner_link_lock synchronization against USB device removal. The \"typec\" symlinks and usb2/3_dev mechanism — did they even exist in 6.17 to begin with? The user's WARN proves the removal of \"typec\" existed. Given that class.c in this tree is a continuation of the 6.17 code (class.c existed in 6.17 — I'm now fairly confident that 6.17 had drivers/usb/typec/class.c? Hmm, actually no! In 6.17 did typec_register_partner live in drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c?...).\n\nUgh. Let me settle it with the tree: is there any evidence in this tree of what 6.17 was like? No — this is 7.x.\n\nBut actually, wait. Reconsidering: 6.17's class.c did exist: I remember the files in mainline 6.17's drivers/usb/typec/: \"altmodes/, class.c?\"... I have a vague memory of reviewing 6.1x trees, and typec/ containing: anx7411.c, hd3ss3220.c, mux/, rt1719.c, stusb160x.c, tipd/, tcpm/, ucsi/, wusb3801.c, port.h, typec.h, pd.c? ... honestly I can't recall whether class.c existed in 6.17.\n\nThat said, the OEM kernel 6.17.0-1011-oem is an \"Ubuntu OEM flavor\" (a kernel for Dell/HP/Lenovo laptops built by Canonical, tracking mainline 6.17). The user is asking to find the root cause in the kernel source and whether the fix is in this tree or upstream. The deliverable: identify the problematic function and the ENOENT mechanism, and report the upstream fix (commit/series).\n\nI can't verify the exact 6.17 file layout, but I can describe it generically: \"the typec partner core (in 6.17 in drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c, now in class.c) and the ucsi driver.\"\n\nHmm — no, wait. I should double-check whether 6.17 even had this \"typec\" symlink feature in the first place, or whether 6.17 had some other \"typec\" name. Let me think about the \"kernfs: can not remove 'typec'\" bug reports on LKML in 2025... There was one! I recall a 2025 bug report about a Dell notebook with a warning on boot — \"kernfs: can not remove 'typec', no directory,\" and Heikki Krogerus's reply, with a fix series in 2025-10/11 for 6.17 RC or 6.18: \"typec: Fix partner's USB device link removal\" hmm...\n\nActually, I recall something more concrete: the upstream fix is:\n\ncommit 0b7a17939d4c9841e452c97f77d3c31b64a3a1a2? No, I can't fabricate a hash.\n\nOK: the user's expectation is to \"report the upstream fix (c