ies) if present, citing file:line evidence.\" Based on the tree evidence, I can identify the fix by content:\n- The partner_link_lock serialization in class.c (register @1106/1149-1162, unregister @1174/1183-1194, attach @2143/2151-2166, deattach @2168/2174-2185) + the USB-side hook in hub.c (attach @2722 after enumeration; deattach @2363 before device_del @2376).\n- Whether this is a new fix in 7.x: the existence of port.c/hub.c changes + typec_connector in include/linux/usb/typec.h suggests the \"USB port object + typec connector\" series is 7.x-era. In 6.17, the USB core did not have struct usb_port with connector components?? Hmm — but wait: did 6.17's hub.c have \"usb_port\" objects? I honestly believe the USB port device (struct usb_port, port_dev->dev, /sys/bus/usb/devices/usb1-port2? Hmm the \"ports\" attribute) appeared around 2024 (6.10?) — \"usb: core: Introduce usb_port object\" by? — the \"hub_port_xxx\" refactor. And I think hub.c's `sysfs_create_link(&port_dev->dev.kobj, ...)` \"device\"/\"port\" symlinks were in the 6.14 era. So the port object is old (≤6.17). What's new: binding to TYPEC \"connector\" and the typec_attach hook.\n\nSo in 6.17, how did the typec core link the partner to the usb device without USB-core hooks?? It couldn't know. Unless UCSI/tcpm itself passed the usb device when registering the partner: 6.17's typec_partner_desc didn't have an attach callback either... I don't think 6.17's typec_register_partner_desc had usb2/usb3 at all!\n\nTherefore in 6.17, the \"typec\" in the WARN must have a different origin!! Let me rethink. 6.17, kernfs_remove_by_name_ns with \"typec\"...\n\nWhat if the WARN in 6.17 comes from removal of the PARTNER device itself? Device name \"typec-partner-...\", no, that's not it.\n\nWhat about `typec_port`'s sysfs directory: /sys/class/typec/typec0 — device \"typec0.\" kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(parent=kernfs_root's class directory? name=\"typec\")? That would remove the CLASS's kobj... \"can not remove 'typec