num typec_data_role data_role;\n\tbool is_rtd;\n\tunsigned num_altmodes;\n\tconst struct typec_altmode_desc *altmodes;\n\tbool usb_power_delivery;\n\tenum usb_pd_revision pd_revision;\n\tbool accessory;\n\tenum typec_swmn_version?? no\n\tenum usb_capability usb_capability;\n\tconst struct usb_pd_identity *identity;\n};\n```\n\nHmm, I feel like \"usb_capability\" was added recently (2025? \"typec: Add USB capability\" — for indicating usb2/usb3). And `typec_partner.set_usb_mode` \"typec_partner_set_usb_mode\"?? In this tree's class.c exports (from the EXPORT list): typec_partner_set_usb_mode! Is typec_partner_set_usb_mode new in 7.x? Or 6.17? Hmm.\n\nTime check — I've been going deep for a while. Let me refocus the deliverable:\n\n1. **Root cause chain with file:line (in this tree)**:\n   - WARN is at fs/kernfs/dir.c (kernfs_remove_by_name_ns, ~line 1706 in 6.17; here: verify the line) — fires when the child dentry isn't found under a parent that isn't a valid directory → ENOENT.\n   - Caller: sysfs_remove_link() (fs/sysfs/symlink.c).\n   - Offending call: drivers/usb/typec/class.c:909 `sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \"typec\")` inside typec_partner_unlink_device(), line 906.\n   - typec_partner_unlink_device is called from typec_unregister_partner() (class.c:1174; call sites :1185, :1189 guarded by port->usb2/3_dev pointers).\n   - typec_unregister_partner ← ucsi_unregister_partner (ucsi/ucsi.c:1138; :1151) ← ucsi_handle_connector_change (ucsi/ucsi.c:1264; call sites ~:1325) on workqueue kworker — matches the user's stack.\n\n2. **Why ENOENT**: dev = port->usb2_dev/usb3_dev (USB device). If that USB device's kobj directory has already been destroyed (device_del'd by the hub on disconnect) or was never created, kernfs_find_and_get_by_name(parent, \"typec\") fails → WARN + ENOENT. 6.17's race: typec partner unregistration (ucsi workqueue on CC/PD change, e.g. a transient cable bounce at boot) races with USB disconnect: hub cycled the port while the partner was still half-registered/unlinked; port->usbX_dev was non-NULL and pointed at a dead kobj. In 6.17, unlink was not serialized with USB-side attach/detach, and the sysfs_remove_link was done unconditionally. Also the double-removal case: if register failed after link creation... etc.\n\n3. **Fix**: a series introducing typec_connector attach/deattach with a dedicated lock:\n   - include/linux/usb/typec.h: typec_connector and inline functions typec_attach/typec_deattach.\n   - drivers/usb/typec/class.c: typec_partner_link_device (888) / typec_partner_unlink_device (906); partner_link_lock in typec_register_partner (:1149-1162) and typec_unregister_partner (:1183-1194) — clear the pointer while holding the lock and also before device_unregister; typec_partner_attach (:214x) / typec_partner_deattach (:216x).\n   - drivers/usb/core/hub.c: typec_deattach (:2363) strictly before device_del (:2376) in usb_disconnect(); typec_attach (:2722) after enumeration.\n   - drivers/usb/core/port.c: connector_bind/unbind (component framework) wiring USB port ↔ typec connector.\n\n   This is a 7.x (or 6.18+?) series — \"is the fix already in this tree?\" YES (this tree contains exactly this mechanism). Is it in \"upstream\"? = mainline ≥ the version this tree is (7.x). For 6.17: not backported unless stable took it — this is a restructuring (new API), unlikely to be cleanly backported; so for Canonical OEM, it should be fixed in the 7.0-based OEM kernel, or needs a targeted backport fix.\n\nBut I can't give a commit hash with confidence. I should honestly state that I can't cite the exact commit (no git access), and instead identify the fix by file:line in this tree, and (if I can remember the series name) — the series was probably by Heikki Krogerus \"typec: Fix partner link removal race\" hmm. Don't fabricate a hash. But the user is asking to \"report the upstream fix (commit/series) if it exists.\" Should I present the mechanism + say it's included in this tree (kernel 7.x, so mainline 7.0) an