{"type":"turn_end","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Not there either. So where is the component master that binds the connector? connector_bind(struct device *dev, struct device *connector, void *data) — is it master_bind? component master: `component_match_add`, or the master calls `component_bind_all`... The signature of the `.bind` callback: `int (*bind)(struct device *dev, struct device *master, void *data)` — the second argument is the master. So the master here is the TYPEC CONNECTOR device. Who is adding it as master? Probably within drivers/usb/typec/class.c, via `component_` — grep for \"component\" in class.c: no matches... Let me look at the include of \"linux/component.h\" and the other files in drivers/usb/typec: pd.c? bus.c? Also, could the binding instead be a BUS match (does \"connector_bind\" get triggered by the device link of a sysfs symlink?). Hmm — this is a component_match that uses dev_name: does the usb port look for the typec connector via ACPI or via the port number's sysfs \"typec\" hint?\n\nActually — wait, maybe in the bind() signature, \"struct device *connector\" IS the master itself. The master = typec connector. The master is registered by `component_add`... no wait, the master = `component_register`/`component_add`? No: the master uses the component_match_* + component_bind_all API, and the component side uses component_add. So the TYPEC PORT driver (class.c's typec_register_port) should be the one doing component_register... but class.c has no \"component\". Hmm!\n\nOr maybe the MASTER is the USB PORT: connector_ops.bind(dev=port?, connector=?, data) — in bind_all, the callback receives (subdev, master). In the case of component_add(&port_dev->dev, &connector_ops): port_dev is the COMPONENT, and \"connector\" is the master. So master = typec connector: registered by whom? grep for \"component\" inside the include file? Let me grep for \"component\" in the other typec files and in include:","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_y7ifhphy","name":"rtk","arguments":{"path":"drivers/usb/typec/pd.c","pattern":"component"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_9tdqw1pp","name":"rtk","arguments":{"path":"include/linux/usb/typec.h","pattern":"component"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_thdyje5m","name":"rtk","arguments":{"path":"drivers/usb/typec/port-mapper.c","pattern":"component"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"ollama","model":"qwen3.8:27b-q8_0","usage":{"input":106990,"output":586,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":107576,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787791950054,"responseId":"chatcmpl-885","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_y7ifhphy","toolName":"rtk","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Command failed: rtk grep component drivers/usb/typec/pd.c"}],"details":{"exitCode":1},"isError":false,"timestamp":1787791982308},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_9tdqw1pp","toolName":"rtk","content":[{"type":"text","text":" * enumerated, USB3 components (retimers, phys, and what have you) do not need\n"}],"details":{},"isError":false,"timestamp":1787791982308},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_thdyje5m","toolName":"rtk","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#include <linux/component.h>\n\treturn component_bind_all(dev, &port->con);\n\tcomponent_unbind_all(dev, &port->con);\nstatic const struct component_master_ops typec_aggregate_ops = {\n\tstruct component_match *match;\n\t\tcomponent_match_add(&arg->port->dev, &arg->match, typec_port_compare,\n\t\t\tcomponent_match_add(&arg->port->dev, &arg->match,\n\t * REVISIT: Now each connector can have only a single component master.\n\t * the connector, every one of those needs to have its own component\n\t * master, because each different type of component needs to be bind to\n\t * the connector independently of the other components. That requires\n\t * i