irectory\") and returns ENOENT.\n- The caller here is the sysfs link removal: fs/sysfs/symlink.c:143 sysfs_remove_link() → kernfs_remove_by_name(parent, name) (fs/sysfs/symlink.c:152), which is ns → :135 kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(kobj->sd, name, ns).\n- \"name\" == \"typec\" ⇒ the failing entry is a sysfs symlink literally named \"typec\".\n\n## 2. The problematic functions (drivers/usb/typec[+ucsi])\n- 'typec' symlink: created in typec_partner_link_device() drivers/usb/typec/class.c:888 — line 892 sysfs_create_link(&dev->kobj, &partner->dev.kobj, \"typec\"); where dev = the port's USB2/USB3 device (port->usb2_dev / port->usb3_dev, class.c:1158-1161); the reverse link is at 896.\n- Removal: typec_partner_unlink_device() class.c:906-913: 908 removes the reverse link, 909 'sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \"typec\");' — exactly the entry named in the WARN.\n- Caller of unlink: typec_unregister_partner() class.c:1174 (defined at 1174, exports at 1196): under port->partner_link_lock (1183) it does class.c:1185 typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, port->usb2_dev), :1189 usb3, then class.c:1193 device_unregister. The only guards are 'if (port->usb2_dev)' (1184) / 'if (port->usb3_dev)' (1188).\n- UCSI side: ucsi_unregister_partner() drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1138 → :1151 typec_unregister_partner(con->partner) → :1153 con->partner = NULL. Its caller: ucsi_handle_connector_change() ucsi/ucsi.c:1264 (work 'con->work', queued at boot on cable/PD state change; unregister call at ucsi/ucsi.c:1325, register at 1313) — matches the user's stack (kworker/6:0, events WQ). ucsi_check_connection() ucsi/ucsi.c:1201 is another caller (1215 register / 1221 unregister), and ucsi_init's probe failure path is :1912.\n\n## 3. Why ENOENT ('no directory')\n- dev->kobj is the kobject of a USB device (the hub's child port device). kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(parent=dev's dentry, \"typec\") requires the parent dentry to be alive and to be a directory. If the hub has already torn down the USB device (