B-side hook existed… wait — hub.c in 6.17 mainline: did the \"usb_port object\" with connector_bind and typec_attach exist in 6.17?? \"usb_hub_create_port_device\" \"usb_port\" objects — is that from 6.13+? (the \"usb: core: Introduce port object\" change was merged around 2023-2024, 6.7/6.8?) Hmm! The USB port object (struct usb_port with its own kobj \"/sys/bus/usb/devices/usbX-port-Y\"?) — I have no memory of that being in 6.17. In 6.17, /sys/bus/usb/devices/ had hub/port devices via the old \"port\" sysfs entry, not a separate port object… The component_add/connector binding mechanism is fairly new (2025). And the 7.0 USB-core reorganization (usbcore merge, devices.c, port.c) suggests a major overhaul in 7.x.\n\nSo how the \"typec\" symlink mechanism worked in 6.17: in 6.17, I think the mechanism in class.c was like this: typec_link_ports? Hmm.\n\nNo, let me step back and reconsider. I may have been overthinking: 6.17 OEM = basically mainline 6.17. User's stack: ucsi_handle_connector_change → ucsi_unregister_partner → typec_unregister_partner → … → kernfs WARN 'typec'. In 6.17, the \"typec\" symlinks were on the usb2/usb3 devices, and I think there was no partner_link_lock, and there was no typec_attach/deattach in the USB core either! Then how did 6.17's typec core find the usb devices??\n\nAh, wait. Now I remember!! In 6.17, was it the UCSI driver itself that looked up the USB port and usb2/usb3 devices? No… hmm, 6.17's drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c…\n\nActually. Let me recall the 2025-02 commit by Heikki Krogerus: \"typec: Add typec_connector to sysfs\"… and \"usb: core: Add a 'connector' symlink to the USB port\"… that was the one that added symlinks to the connector for user space (the udev/udisks thing): the USB port object got a \"connector\" symlink pointing to /sys/class/typec/typecX. That feature: \"usb: core: Allow looking up the USB port device from a USB device\" hmm.\n\nAnd crucially, the typec side: 6.17's class.c had typec_partner_link_device(partner, dev)?? Wait, but that function's signature takes a USB device directly… In 6.17, when did the partner get linked to the usb device? Hmm — maybe 6.17 didn't have \"typec\" symlinks on the USB devices at all, and the 'typec' in the WARN is something completely different!\n\nAlternative hypothesis: in 6.17, the \"typec\" name might come from the TYPEC PORT's class: /sys/class/typec/typec0 — no wait.\n\nHmm, what about \"typec_register_usb2_port\"? In 6.17's drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c there was this:\n\nHmm, hold on, I now really remember that 6.17's tcpm.c had a function (added around ~6.14, 2024, by Heikki — commit \"typec: tcpm: Add USB2/USB3 symlinks to the partner\", or as part of \"typec: Add partner's USB capability\"):\n\n6.17's drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c (I've seen this file):\n```c\nint typec_register_partner(struct typec_port *port, struct typec_partner_desc *desc)\n...\nstatic void typec_partner_plug(struct typec_partner *partner)\n{\n\tstruct typec_port *port = partner->port;\n\tint ret;\n\n\tlockdep_assert_held(&port->lock);\n\n\tret = dev_set_name(&partner->dev, \"%s-partner-%d\",\n\t\t\t   dev_name(&port->dev), port->id++);\n\tif (ret < 0)\n\t\treturn;\n\n\tpartner->dev.bus = &typec_bus_type;\n\tpartner->dev.type = &typec_partner_type;\n\tpartner->dev.parent = port->dev.parent;\n\tpartner->dev.groups = usb_pd_id_groups? \n\tpartner->dev.release = typec_partner_release;\n\n\tret = device_add(&partner->dev);\n\t...\n}\n```\n\nI can't verify whether 6.17 had typec/usb symlinks. But the very existence of the user's WARN proves there was a name \"typec\" that got removed via kernfs_remove_by_name_ns. The only place in the typec code (in any of these trees) where \"typec\" is used as a sysfs entry name is this symlink mechanism (class.c:892/898/909 — probably identical in 6.17 too). I'm now fairly confident the \"typec\" symlink feature has been in class.c since 6.14/6.15 (\"typec: Add 'typec' entry to USB devices\" — added so that user space can find a typec partner from a USB device), and in 6.17 the USB side had the USB port object (struct usb_port; /sys/bus/usb/devices/…-ports/…) — hmm, no, wait.\n\nWell, at this point, from a \"find the root cause\" perspective, the exact internal details of 6.17 vs 7.x matter less than the following:\n1. The chain: the kernfs WARN comes from sysfs_remove_link (fs/sysfs/symlink.c) → when the parent dentry no longer has that child / is not a directory, it does a WARN + ENOENT inside kernfs_remove_by_name_ns (fs/kernfs/dir.c:17xx).\n2. The failing sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \"typec\") is at drivers/usb/typec/class.c:909 (typec_partner_unlink_device), called from typec_unregister_partner (class.c:1185/1189; in 6.17 these lines live in tcpm.c/the equivalent 6.17 file), triggered from ucsi_unregister_partner (ucsi/ucsi.c:1138 → typec_unregister_partner at 1151), which is called from ucsi_handle_connector_change (ucsi/ucsi.c:1264, the call at ~1325) running on a workqueue (kworker), which is exactly as the user's stack says.\n3. Race: while the ucsi connector-change work unregisters the partner (or after the register fails), the USB core is concurrently removing the USB port's child device (or the USB device was never attached). port->usb2/3_dev points to a device whose kobj dentry is gone → ENOENT. There is no synchronization between the partner's unlink and the USB device's removal.\n4. Fix in this tree (i.e., the upstream fix series): a locking scheme (partner_link_lock), where the USB side (drivers/usb/core/hub.c:2363 deattach before device removal; :2722 attach after add) performs the unlink while the device still exists, clearing the pointer under the same lock. Also the register path defers link handling to after device_register success; the ucsi side too…\n\nHmm, wait — but is partner_link_lock alone a \"fix\"? If device_unregister(&partner->dev) is also under the lock, and in hub.c deattach runs before usb device deletion, then at the time the ucsi work runs, either (a) deattach hasn't run: usbX_dev is non-NULL, the device is alive → unlink OK; (b) deattach has run (before usb removal): pointer cleared → skipped. Either way, there is no unlink against a dead kobj. So the race is fixed — provided hub.c:2363 is on the device deletion path before deletion. Need to check the context of 2363: which function it's in, and the position relative to usb_put_dev. Also need to check whether deattach is missing on some path (e.g., hub removal: the usb_hub_remove_port_device path).\n\nLet me use the hub.c oracle to find the function containing line 2363: grep hub.c for the function name around there: pattern \"hub_port_connect\" \"usb_disconnect\" \"unregister\" etc. The oracle 1787791312 only has lines containing \"port_dev\". To get the line number of the function definition: grep hub.c for \"static void usb_port\" and \"void usb_disconnect\".","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_rf4mmq2w","name":"rtk","arguments":{"path":"drivers/usb/core/hub.c","pattern":"void usb_disconnect"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ifwc0bpq","name":"rtk","arguments":{"path":"drivers/usb/core/hub.c","pattern":"static void hub_port_disconnect"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_f6ej2co0","name":"rtk","arguments":{"path":"drivers/usb/core/hub.c","pattern":"hub_port_connect"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"ollama","model":"qwen3.8:27b-q8_0","usage":{"input":90777,"output":2525,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":93302,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787791326115,"responseId":"chatcmpl-649","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_rf4mmq2w","toolName":"rtk","content":[{"type":"text","text":"void usb_disconnect(struct usb_device **pdev)\n"}],"details":{},"isError":false,"timestamp":1787791458612},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_ifwc0bpq","toolName":"rtk","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Command failed: rtk grep stati