st: unregister unlinks + NULLs both pointers under the lock → deattach sees NULL pointer and skips (2181 \"if (port->usb2_dev == dev) port->usb2_dev = NULL;\" — does it call unlink first at 2176-2178? From oracle :21[0-9][0-9]: 2174 lock; 2175 partner = typec_get_partner; 2177 typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, dev); 2181 if usb2_dev==dev → NULL. Hmm, line 2177 unlinks without a NULL check?! Is 2177 conditioned on `if (partner)`? Let me check lines 2168-2187 in detail. Query oracle 1787790696 (\"port\" log) with \":21[6-8][0-9]:\".","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_icd8714b","name":"rtk","arguments":{"path":"/root/.local/share/rtk/tee/1787790696_grep_0_drivers_usb_typec_class_c.log","pattern":":21[4-9][0-9]:"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"ollama","model":"qwen3.8:27b-q8_0","usage":{"input":94936,"output":697,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":95633,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1787791492646,"responseId":"chatcmpl-622","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_icd8714b","toolName":"rtk","content":[{"type":"text","text":"drivers/usb/typec/class.c:2146:struct typec_port *port = container_of(con, struct typec_port, con);\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2151:mutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2154:port->usb2_dev = dev;\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2157:port->usb3_dev = dev;\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2160:partner = typec_get_partner(port);\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2166:mutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2171:struct typec_port *port = container_of(con, struct typec_port, con);\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2174:mutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2175:partner = typec_get_partner(port);\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2181:if (port->usb2_dev == dev)\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2182:port->usb2_dev = NULL;\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2183:else if (port->usb3_dev == dev)\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2184:port->usb3_dev = NULL;\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2185:mutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2189:* typec_set_data_role - Report data role change\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2190:* @port: The USB Type-C Port where the role was changed\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2193:* This routine is used by the port drivers to report data role changes.\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2195:void typec_set_data_role(struct typec_port *port, enum typec_data_role role)\ndrivers/usb/typec/class.c:2199:if (port->data_role == role)\n"}],"details":{},"isError":false,"timestamp":1787791530227},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The body of deattach (2168~2187, lines containing \"port\" have been confirmed; line 2177's unlink contains partner but not \"port\" — from the earlier \"link\" oracle: line 2177 has typec_partner_unlink_device(partner, dev)); probably:\n\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_deattach(struct typec_connector *con, struct device *dev)\n{\n\tstruct typec_port *port = container_of(con, struct typec_port, con);\n\tstruct typec_partner *partner;\n\n\tmutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);\n\tpartner = typec_get_partner(port);\n\tif (partner)\n\t\ttypec_partner_unlink_device(partner, dev);   // 2176-2177\n\tif (port->usb2_dev == dev)\n\t\tport->usb2_dev = NULL;                       // 2181-2182\n\telse if (port->usb3_dev == dev)\n\t\tport->usb3_dev = NULL;                       // 2183-2184\n\tmutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);\n}\n```\n\nAnd attach (2143~2167):\n```c\nstatic void typec_partner_attach(struct typec_connector *con, struct device *dev)\n{\n\tstruct typec_port *port = container_of(con, struct typec_port, con);\n\tstruct typec_partner *partner;\n\tbool is_usb3 = is_usb3? (dev...);\n\n\tif (is_usb3?) {\n\t\t...\n\t}\n\tmutex_lock(&port->partner_link_lock);\n\tport->usb2_dev = dev;     // 2154\n\t... (2155-2156)\n\tport->usb3_dev = dev;     // 2157\n\tpartner = typec_get_partner(port);\n\tif (partner)                          // 2161?\n\t\ttypec_partner_link_device(partner, dev);  // 2163\n\tmutex_unlock(&port->partner_link_lock);  // 2166\n}\n```\n\nNow — the key point — is the fix for the user's 6.17 bug in this tree? Comparison with 6.17: in 6.17 (the user's kernel), the flow had no partner_link_lock synchronization against USB device removal. The \"typec\" symlinks and usb2/3_dev mechanism — did they even exist in 6.17 to begin with? The user's WARN proves the removal of \"typec\" existed. Given that class.c in this tree is a continuation of the 6.17 code (class.c existed in 6.17 — I'm now fairly confident that 6.17 had drivers/usb/typec/class.c? Hmm, actually no! In 6.17 did typec_register_partner live in drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c?...).\n\nUgh. Let me settle it with the tree: is there any evidence in this tree of what 6.17 was like? No — this is 7.x.\n\nBut actually, wait. Reconsidering: 6.17's class.c did exist: I remember the files in mainline 6.17's drivers/usb/typec/: \"altmodes/, class.c?\"... I have a vague memory of reviewing 6.1x trees, and typec/ containing: anx7411.c, hd3ss3220.c, mux/, rt1719.c, stusb160x.c, tipd/, tcpm/, ucsi/, wusb3801.c, port.h, typec.h, pd.c? ... honestly I can't recall whether class.c existed in 6.17.\n\nThat said, the OEM kernel 6.17.0-1011-oem is an \"Ubuntu OEM flavor\" (a kernel for Dell/HP/Lenovo laptops built by Canonical, tracking mainline 6.17). The user is asking to find the root cause in the kernel source and whether the fix is in this tree or upstream. The deliverable: identify the problematic function and the ENOENT mechanism, and report the upstream fix (commit/series).\n\nI can't verify the exact 6.17 file layout, but I can describe it generically: \"the typec partner core (in 6.17 in drivers/usb/typec/tcpm.c, now in class.c) and the ucsi driver.\"\n\nHmm — no, wait. I should double-check whether 6.17 even had this \"typec\" symlink feature in the first place, or whether 6.17 had some other \"typec\" name. Let me think about the \"kernfs: can not remove 'typec'\" bug reports on LKML in 2025... There was one! I recall a 2025 bug report about a Dell notebook with a warning on boot — \"kernfs: can not remove 'typec', no directory,\" and Heikki Krogerus's reply, with a fix series in 2025-10/11 for 6.17 RC or 6.18: \"typec: Fix partner's USB device link removal\" hmm...\n\nActually, I recall something more concrete: the upstream fix is:\n\ncommit 0b7a17939d4c9841e452c97f77d3c31b64a3a1a2? No, I can't fabricate a hash.\n\nOK: the user's expectation is to \"report the upstream fix (commit/series) if present, citing file:line evidence.\" Based on the tree evidence, I can identify the fix by content:\n- The partner_link_lock serialization in class.c (register @1106/1149-1162, unregister @1174/1183-1194, attach @2143/2151-2166, deattach @2168/2174-2185) + the USB-side hook in hub.c (attach @2722 after enumeration; deattach @2363 before device_del @2376).\n- Whether this is a new fix in 7.x: the existence of port.c/hub.c changes + typec_connector in include/linux/usb/typec.h suggests the \"USB port object + typec connector\" series is 7.x-era. In 6.17, the USB core did not have struct usb_port with connector components?? Hmm — but wait: did 6.17's hub.c have \"usb_port\" objects? I honestly believe the USB port device (struct usb_port, port_dev->dev, /sys/bus/usb/devices/usb1-port2? Hmm the \"ports\" attribute) appeared around 2024 (6.10?) — \"usb: core: Introduce usb_port object\" by? — the \"hub_port_xxx\" refactor. And I think hub.c's `sysfs_create_link(&port_dev->dev.kobj, ...)` \"device\"/\"port\" symlinks were in the 6.14 era. So the port object is old (≤6.17). What's new: binding to TYPEC \"connector\" and the typec_attach hook.\n\nSo in 6.17, how did the typec core link the partner to the usb device without USB-core hooks?? It couldn't know. Unless UCSI/tcpm itself passed the usb device when registering the partner: 6.17's