s running has the pre-fix code (this is a restructuring — new component framework wiring + locks — and has not been cleanly backported to 6.17 stable as far as I can confirm from the tree).\n  - I should be honest about my inability to identify a specific commit/series. But... hmm. The user explicitly asks \"if the upstream fix (commit/series) exists, report it, citing file:line evidence for each step\". What I can cite is file:line evidence inside this tree. For the commit, do I note the likely series (Heikki Krogerus, the \"typec: Rework partner/cable handling\" for 7.0 + USB port/connector component binding) with an honest caveat?\n\nLet me do one more memory probe for the actual commit: do I genuinely recall a commit \"typec: Fix removing the 'typec' link\"... no. What I really do remember is an LKML series from October 2025 by Heikki Krogerus: \"[PATCH v2 00/17] typec: Rework of the partner/plug/cable sysfs\"?? Hmm — plausible given a \"REVISIT\" comment in port-mapper: \"Now each connector can only have one component master...\" — I think that comment is from an actual commit in this series (Heikki, 2025). But I'm not confident enough to state a hash or even the exact series title. I'll write it this way: series = the typec \"connector\"/component-based rework (7.0, Heikki Krogerus's typec core rework + USB port connector binding), no verified hash.\n\nAlso, regarding 6.17 file names: caveat: I should avoid asserting the 6.17 file layout I can't verify. I'll write: \"in 6.17, the equivalent functions are in the typec partner core (same code, pre-restructure) and in drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c (ucsi/ subdirectory exists since 6.14)\".\n\nHmm — one last sanity check on the ucsi.c call sites: the user's stack says \"typec_unregister_partner → ucsi_unregister_partner inside ucsi_handle_connector_change\". The unregister at ucsi/ucsi.c:1325 is inside ucsi_handle_connector_change (1264-~1340) — verify it's really inside: the next static after 1264 is ucsi_reset_connector @1397 → yes, 1325 ∈ [1264, 1396]. ✓\nAnd :1221 is inside ucsi_check_connection [1201,1226] ✓, :1313 is also inside handle_connector_change ✓.\n\nAlso, re-check the line numbers for the WARN in the user's kernel: \"fs/kernfs/dir.c:1706\" in 6.17 OEM — the WARN line in 6.17 mainline's kernfs dir.c is at 1706 — consistent with this tree's 1731 (shift of +25 lines).\n\nWriting the report now. Keep it organized, cite file:line for each step, honestly state what I verified and what I couldn't (commit hash).","thinkingSignature":"reasoning"},{"type":"text","text":"I have the complete chain verified against the source. Here's the full analysis.\n\n# Root cause: `kernfs: can not remove 'typec', no directory`\n\n## 1. Where the warning comes from (kernfs)\n\nThe WARN text in your 6.17 OEM kernel (`fs/kernfs/dir.c:1706`) is the one in `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns()` — verified in this tree at:\n\n- `fs/kernfs/dir.c:1724` — `int kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(struct kernfs_node *parent, const char *name, ...)`\n- `fs/kernfs/dir.c:1731-1732` — `WARN(1, KERN_WARNING \"kernfs: can not remove '%s', no directory\\n\", name)` + returns `-ENOENT`\n\nThe function looks the entry up **by name under an already-existing parent directory dentry**. It WARNs+fails with ENOENT when the parent dentry is no longer a live directory (already torn down / never created), so the child name can't be resolved. Your `name` is **`typec`** — a sysfs symlink literally called `typec`.\n\nThe caller is the sysfs link-removal path, not a direct kernfs call:\n\n- `fs/sysfs/symlink.c:143` — `void sysfs_remove_link(struct kobject *kobj, const char *name)`\n- `fs/sysfs/symlink.c:152` / `:135` — `kernfs_remove_by_name(parent, name)` / `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(kobj->sd, name, ns)`\n\n## 2. The exact offending functions (drivers/usb/typec & .../ucsi)\n\nThe `typec` symlink is the link the Type-C core creates **on a USB port's child (usb2/usb3) device** pointing at the Type-C partner device:\n\n| Step | Function | Location |\n|---|---|---|\n| Create `typec` link | `typec_partner_link_device()` | `drivers/usb/typec/class.c:888`; link at `class.c:892` (`sysfs_create_link(&dev->kobj, &partner->dev.kobj, \"typec\")`), reverse link at `:896` |\n| **Remove `typec` link (the failing call)** | `typec_partner_unlink_device()` | `drivers/usb/typec/class.c:906`; **`class.c:909`** — `sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \"typec\");` |\n| Unlink + kill partner | `typec_unregister_partner()` | `drivers/usb/typec/class.c:1174` (export `:1196`); unlinks at `class.c:1185` (usb2_dev) and `:1189` (usb3_dev), `device_unregister(&partner->dev)` at `class.c:1193`; only guard is `if (port->usb2/usb3_dev)` at `:1184/:1188` |\n| UCSI glue | `ucsi_unregister_partner()` | `drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1138`; calls `typec_unregister_partner(con->partner)` at `ucsi/ucsi.c:1151`, clears `con->partner` at `:1153` |\n| Boot-time trigger | `ucsi_handle_connector_change()` (work on `con->work`) | `drivers/usb/typec/ucsi/ucsi.c:1264`; the unregister call is `ucsi/ucsi.c:1325` (register at `:1313`) — runs from `kworker/X:0` on the `events` workqueue, exactly your stack |\n\nThis matches your trace precisely: `ucsi_handle_connector_change` (kworker) → `ucsi_unregister_partner` (`ucsi/ucsi.c:1138`) → `typec_unregister_partner` (`class.c:1174`) → `typec_partner_unlink_device` (`class.c:906`) → `sysfs_remove_link` on a `dev` whose kobj directory is gone.\n\n(A secondary registrar exists: `ucsi_check_connection()`, `ucsi/ucsi.c:1201`, registers at `:1215` / unregisters at `:1221` via the `ucsi_partner_task` delayed work — same code path.)\n\n## 3. Why it fails with ENOENT on this Dell machine\n\n- `dev` in `typec_partner_unlink_device()` is the port's **USB device** (`port->usb2_dev`/`port->usb3_dev`). `kernfs_remove_by_name_ns(&dev's dentry, \"typec\")` requires that dentry to still be a directory.\n- The UCSI firmware on this platform (BIOS 0.4.53) reports a transient connect→disconnect bounce at boot. `ucsi_handle_connector_change` runs on a kworker and **races the USB core's hub disconnect** which removes the USB device (`device_del(&udev->dev)`, `drivers/usb/core/hub.c:2376`).\n- In the 6.17-era code the unlink of the `typec` link is done purely from the Type-C side, guarded only by the `port->usb2/usb3_dev` pointer, with **no lock synchronizing it with the USB core attaching/detaching the device**. So the kworker can reach `sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, \"typec\")` after the hub has already dropped that kobj (or the USB device never finished registering in the first place during the bounce) → lookup fails → WARN at `dir.c:1706` (6.17) + `-ENOENT`.\n\n## 4. Is the fix already present — in this tree and upstream?\n\nThis tree is **mainline 7.0** (`Makefile: VERSION = 7, PATCHLEVEL = 0`; origin `torvalds/linux`, master `66498c75b4f8`), i.e. newer than your 6.17 OEM kernel. It contains the reworked design that eliminates this race:\n\n1. **A dedicated lock serializes every mutation of the partner↔USB-device links** — `port->partner_link_lock`, initialized at `drivers/usb/typec/class.c:2746`:\n   - register: `class.c:1149-1162` (`device_register(&partner->dev)` at `:1150`, links created *after* successful register at `:1158-1161`)\n   - unregister: `class.c:1183-1194` (link removal at `:1185/:1189` **plus clearing the `port->usb2/3_dev` pointers under the same lock** at `:1186/:1190`, before `device_unregister` at `:1193`)\n2. **The USB core now participates symmetrically in the link lifecycle** (new API in `include/linux/usb/typec.h`: `struct typec_connector`, `typec_attach()`/`typec_deattach()`):\n   - attach at enumeration: `drivers/usb/core/hub.c:2722` → `typec_partner_attach()` `class.c:2146-2166` (sets `usb2/3_dev` at `:2154/:2157`, link at `:2163`, under the lock)\n   - detach on disconnect: `usb_disconnect()` (`hub.c:2315`) calls **`hub.c:2363` `typec_deattach(...)` strictly *before* the device is deleted at `hub.c:2376`** → `typec_partner_deattach()` `class.c:2171-2185` (un